INSERT INTO cases (title, specialty, patient_age, patient_gender, chief_complaint, excerpt, history, examination, investigations, diagnosis, management, discussion, image_url, is_published, is_free) VALUES
('Acute Gastroenteritis (Diarrhoea)', 'Pediatrics', NULL, NULL, 'Diarrhoea, Dehydration, Under-5', 'The single biggest cause of paediatric OPD visits and under-5 mortality risk in Pakistan — assess hydration first.', 'Number and consistency of stools per day, presence of blood/mucus
Vomiting frequency, ability to tolerate oral fluids
Urine output (reduced wet nappies), presence of thirst
Fever, associated symptoms, recent food/water source, contact with similar illness
Immunisation status (rotavirus vaccine), feeding history (breastfed/formula)', 'Assess hydration status: general condition, eyes (sunken), tears, mucous membranes, skin turgor/pinch, capillary refill
Weight (compare to recent record if available) — best single indicator of fluid loss
Check for fever, abdominal distension/tenderness
Assess alertness/responsiveness
Look for signs of severe dehydration: lethargy, very sunken eyes, very slow skin pinch', 'Usually clinical diagnosis — investigations not routinely needed for uncomplicated watery diarrhoea
Stool microscopy/culture if bloody diarrhoea, prolonged (>7 days), or immunocompromised child
Electrolytes if IV fluids needed or severe dehydration
Blood glucose if lethargic (rule out hypoglycaemia)', NULL, 'No dehydration (Plan A): continue feeding/breastfeeding, extra fluids at home, ORS after each loose stool, zinc supplementation, safety-net advice
Some dehydration (Plan B): ORS 75ml/kg over 4 hours in clinic, reassess, then continue as Plan A
Severe dehydration (Plan C): urgent IV fluids (Ringer''s Lactate/Normal Saline) per WHO protocol, admit
Zinc supplementation for all children with diarrhoea (reduces duration and future episodes)
Continue breastfeeding throughout illness — do NOT withhold feeds
Antibiotics ONLY for bloody diarrhoea (dysentery) or specific pathogens identified

PRESCRIPTION:
Oral Rehydration Salts (low-osmolarity) WHO-ORS/Rehydran/ORS-Plus As per WHO plan A/B, after every loose stool (~50-100ml under 2 yrs, 100-200ml over 2 yrs). Give by spoon/cup frequently, not by bottle in large amounts
Zinc Sulfate Zinkid/Zincovit 10 mg OD (<6 months) or 20 mg OD (≥6 months) for 10–14 days. Reduces duration/severity and risk of future episodes
Ciprofloxacin (only if bloody diarrhoea/dysentery) Ciproxin 15 mg/kg BD for 3 days. Only for confirmed/suspected shigella dysentery, not routine watery diarrhoea', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Signs of severe dehydration (lethargic/unconscious, sunken eyes, unable to drink or drinking poorly, skin pinch goes back very slowly) → IV fluids, admit
Bloody diarrhoea (dysentery) → consider Shigella, needs specific antibiotic and closer monitoring
Persistent vomiting preventing oral rehydration
High fever, seizures, or signs of severe systemic illness
Age <3 months with diarrhoea/vomiting — lower threshold for admission

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per WHO/IMCI (Integrated Management of Childhood Illness) guidelines: classify dehydration as none, some, or severe using standardised assessment; treat with Plan A (home, no dehydration), Plan B (ORS in clinic for some dehydration), or Plan C (urgent IV fluids for severe dehydration); low-osmolarity ORS plus zinc supplementation for 10–14 days is standard of care and reduces diarrhoea duration/severity; antibiotics are NOT routinely indicated for watery diarrhoea.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Continue breastfeeding/normal feeding throughout, give ORS after every loose stool, complete the 10–14 day zinc course, return immediately if the child cannot drink, is very sleepy, has blood in stool, or is not passing urine.', NULL, true, false),
('Pneumonia / Acute Respiratory Infection', 'Pediatrics', NULL, NULL, 'Cough, Fast breathing, IMCI', 'Fast breathing in a child is the single most important sign to check — WHO IMCI built its whole classification around it.', 'Cough, fever, difficulty breathing, poor feeding
Duration of symptoms, any noisy breathing (wheeze/stridor)
Immunisation status (pneumococcal, Hib, measles, pertussis vaccines)
Exposure to indoor smoke (chulha/biomass cooking — major risk factor in Pakistan), overcrowding
Previous episodes, nutritional status, HIV status if relevant', 'Count respiratory rate over a full minute (child calm/not crying): fast breathing thresholds — ≥60/min (<2 months), ≥50/min (2-12 months), ≥40/min (1-5 years)
Look for chest indrawing (lower chest wall retraction)
Auscultate for crackles, reduced air entry, bronchial breathing
Check for danger signs: inability to drink/breastfeed, vomiting everything, convulsions, lethargy/unconsciousness, stridor in a calm child, severe malnutrition
Pulse oximetry if available', 'Usually clinical diagnosis using IMCI criteria — respiratory rate and chest indrawing are key
Pulse oximetry wherever available
Chest X-ray if diagnosis uncertain, not improving, or complications suspected (effusion, abscess)
CBC if hospitalised/severe
Not all children need investigations — WHO IMCI emphasises clinical assessment', NULL, 'Fast breathing only, no danger signs: home care with oral amoxicillin, safety-net advice, review in 2–3 days
Chest indrawing or danger sign: give first dose of antibiotic, refer urgently to hospital for IV/IM antibiotics and oxygen
Encourage continued breastfeeding/feeding and fluids
Antipyretics for fever/discomfort
Educate on reducing indoor smoke exposure and completing immunisation schedule

PRESCRIPTION:
Amoxicillin (non-severe, home management) Amoxil/Ospamox/Moxatag 25 mg/kg BD (or per weight band) for 3–5 days. First-line oral agent per WHO for non-severe pneumonia
Ampicillin + Gentamicin (severe, inpatient) Ampicillin + Gentamicin injectable Per weight-based paediatric dosing, IV/IM. Inpatient use for severe pneumonia/danger signs
Paracetamol syrup (for fever/discomfort) Calpol/Panadol Syrup 10-15 mg/kg QDS PRN. Weight-based dosing, use correct measuring device', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Any WHO danger sign (unable to drink, persistent vomiting, convulsions, lethargic/unconscious, stridor at rest) → classify as severe pneumonia/very severe disease, admit urgently
Chest indrawing → severe pneumonia, needs admission and IV/IM antibiotics
SpO2 <90% → urgent oxygen and admission
Age <2 months with fast breathing → always classify as severe, refer/admit

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per WHO IMCI guidelines: classify as ''no pneumonia — cough/cold'' (home care), ''pneumonia'' (fast breathing only, no danger signs — oral amoxicillin, home care with follow-up), or ''severe pneumonia/very severe disease'' (chest indrawing or any danger sign — refer urgently, first dose antibiotic before referral); amoxicillin is first-line oral antibiotic per WHO/UNICEF child pneumonia guidance.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Complete the full antibiotic course, continue breastfeeding/fluids, return immediately if breathing becomes harder, chest indrawing appears, or the child stops feeding — these mean the illness is worsening. Ensure immunisations are up to date.', NULL, true, false),
('Acute Otitis Media', 'Pediatrics', NULL, NULL, 'Ear pain, Fever, Children', 'Ear-pulling and fever in a toddler after a cold — one of the most over-treated common conditions.', 'Ear pain (or ear-pulling/irritability in infants who can''t verbalise), fever, reduced hearing/feeling of blockage
Preceding upper respiratory tract infection symptoms (very common trigger)
Discharge from ear (suggests perforation)
Recurrent episodes, daycare attendance, passive smoke exposure, bottle-feeding while lying flat (risk factors)
Immunisation status (pneumococcal vaccine reduces risk)', 'Otoscopy: bulging, red/inflamed tympanic membrane, loss of light reflex, or perforation with discharge
Check for mastoid tenderness/swelling (concerning for mastoiditis)
Fever, general assessment for systemic illness
Assess hearing if recurrent/chronic (risk of effusion affecting speech development)', 'Usually a clinical diagnosis via otoscopy — no routine investigations needed
Tympanometry if available and diagnosis unclear
Consider audiometry if recurrent/chronic effusion suspected
Swab of discharge only if not settling with standard treatment', NULL, 'Analgesia/antipyretics as first-line for all cases (paracetamol/ibuprofen)
Watchful waiting 2–3 days for most children >2 years with mild/unilateral disease — most resolve spontaneously
Immediate antibiotics if <2 years with bilateral AOM, otorrhoea present, systemically unwell, or high risk (immunocompromised, craniofacial abnormality)
Safety-net advice: return if not improving in 3 days or worsening
Refer to ENT if recurrent, persistent effusion affecting hearing/speech, or complications

PRESCRIPTION:
Paracetamol / Ibuprofen (first-line for all) Calpol/Panadol Syrup/Brufen Syrup Weight-based, QDS PRN. Adequate analgesia is the priority in most cases
Amoxicillin (if antibiotics indicated) Amoxil/Ospamox Syrup 25 mg/kg BD for 5–7 days. First-line if immediate antibiotics are indicated
Co-amoxiclav (if amoxicillin failure/recurrent) Augmentin Syrup Per weight, BD-TDS for 5-7 days. Second-line option', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Mastoid swelling/tenderness, protruding ear → suspect mastoiditis, urgent ENT referral
Facial nerve palsy, severe headache, neck stiffness → suspect intracranial complication, emergency referral
Persistent perforation/discharge beyond 2 weeks → refer ENT
Recurrent AOM (≥3 episodes in 6 months) → ENT referral to consider grommets

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE CKS and AAP (American Academy of Pediatrics) guidelines: most uncomplicated AOM resolves without antibiotics within 3 days; a ''watchful waiting''/delayed prescription approach with analgesia is appropriate for most children >2 years with unilateral, non-severe disease; immediate antibiotics recommended for children <2 years with bilateral AOM, those with otorrhoea, or systemically unwell/high-risk children.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Most ear infections settle on their own within 2–3 days with just pain relief; return if fever persists beyond 3 days, ear swelling develops behind the ear, or discharge continues beyond 2 weeks.', NULL, true, false),
('Measles', 'Pediatrics', NULL, NULL, 'Rash, Vaccine-preventable, Fever', 'Outbreaks still occur in under-vaccinated communities — recognise the Koplik spots before the rash appears.', 'High fever, cough, coryza (runny nose), conjunctivitis (the ''3 C''s'') preceding rash by 2–4 days
Rash onset — typically starts on face/hairline, spreads downward to trunk and limbs
Contact with a similar case, vaccination status (measles vaccine at 9 and 15 months in Pakistan''s EPI schedule)
Poor feeding, irritability
Ask about complications: ear discharge, difficulty breathing, diarrhoea, altered consciousness', 'Koplik spots (small white spots on buccal mucosa) — pathognomonic, appear just before rash
Maculopapular rash starting at hairline/behind ears, spreading cephalocaudally, becoming confluent
Fever, conjunctivitis, coryza
Assess for complications: chest signs (pneumonia — leading cause of measles death), ear exam (otitis media), hydration status, neurological signs (encephalitis, rare)
Check nutritional status — malnourished children have much higher complication/mortality risk', 'Usually a clinical diagnosis in an outbreak setting
Measles IgM serology or PCR for confirmation (important for public health notification)
CBC, chest X-ray if pneumonia suspected
Notify public health authorities — measles is a notifiable disease', NULL, 'Vitamin A supplementation for 2 consecutive days (WHO recommends this for every measles case)
Supportive care: antipyretics, adequate hydration and nutrition, continue feeding
Isolate from other children (highly contagious) until 4 days after rash onset
Treat secondary bacterial complications (pneumonia, otitis media, diarrhoea) with appropriate antibiotics if they arise
Notify public health authorities; encourage catch-up MMR/measles vaccination in unvaccinated contacts/siblings

PRESCRIPTION:
Vitamin A Arovit/Vitamin A capsules (EPI supply) 50,000 IU (<6 months), 100,000 IU (6-12 months), 200,000 IU (>12 months) — day 1 and day 2. Give to every child with measles, regardless of vitamin A status
Paracetamol Syrup Calpol/Panadol Syrup 10–15 mg/kg QDS PRN. For fever/discomfort
Amoxicillin (if secondary bacterial infection e.g. otitis media/pneumonia develops) Amoxil Syrup 25 mg/kg BD for 5–7 days. Only for confirmed bacterial complications, not measles itself', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Difficulty breathing, chest indrawing → measles pneumonia, a leading cause of death, admit
Altered consciousness, seizures, neck stiffness → suspect encephalitis, emergency referral
Severe dehydration from diarrhoea
Corneal clouding/severe eye involvement (risk of blindness, especially with vitamin A deficiency) → urgent vitamin A and ophthalmology input
Malnourished child with measles — much higher risk, manage aggressively and admit if any complication

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per WHO measles case management guidance: give vitamin A on 2 consecutive days to ALL children diagnosed with measles regardless of nutritional status (reduces mortality and eye complications), plus a third dose 4 weeks later if the child is vitamin A deficient; supportive care for fever/hydration; antibiotics only for bacterial complications (pneumonia, otitis media), not for measles itself; report to public health/EPI programme and ensure catch-up MCV vaccination for the community.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Isolate the child until 4 days after rash starts, ensure siblings/contacts are vaccinated, maintain good nutrition and hydration, return urgently if breathing difficulty, drowsiness, or eye problems develop.', NULL, true, false),
('Febrile Seizures', 'Pediatrics', NULL, NULL, 'Seizure, Fever, Reassurance', 'Terrifying for parents but usually benign — the job is to exclude the dangerous mimics.', 'Age of child (typically 6 months–5 years), seizure duration and character (generalised tonic-clonic typical of simple febrile seizure)
Fever onset relative to seizure — often the seizure occurs as temperature rises rapidly
Single vs recurrent seizures within the same illness, duration <15 min (simple) vs >15 min/focal/recurrent within 24h (complex)
Family history of febrile seizures or epilepsy
Preceding illness symptoms — identify the source of fever (viral URI, otitis media, etc.)', 'Full neurological exam once post-ictal state resolves — should return to normal baseline
Look for the source of fever: ENT exam, chest, abdomen, skin, meningeal signs
Assess for signs of CNS infection: neck stiffness, bulging fontanelle (in infants), photophobia, altered consciousness beyond expected post-ictal period
Growth/development assessment if recurrent', 'Simple febrile seizure with clear source of fever and normal exam: usually no investigations needed beyond identifying fever source
Consider blood glucose to exclude hypoglycaemia as a cause
LP indicated if any meningeal signs, age <12 months (lower threshold), or unclear source
EEG/neuroimaging NOT routinely indicated for simple febrile seizures', NULL, 'Reassure parents — simple febrile seizures do not cause brain damage and carry excellent prognosis
Manage the underlying fever source (most often viral URI, otitis media, tonsillitis)
Antipyretics for comfort (do NOT prevent recurrence, but help symptomatic relief)
Educate parents on first-aid for future seizures: place child on side, do not restrain, do not put anything in mouth, time the seizure, seek help if >5 minutes
Admit if complex features, first presentation with diagnostic uncertainty, or social/parental anxiety needs support

PRESCRIPTION:
Paracetamol Syrup (for fever comfort, not seizure prevention) Calpol/Panadol Syrup 10-15 mg/kg QDS PRN. Does not prevent seizure recurrence but improves comfort
Treat underlying source (e.g. Amoxicillin if bacterial infection identified) Amoxil Syrup 25 mg/kg BD for 5-7 days if indicated. Only if a bacterial source is confirmed
Rectal/buccal Diazepam or Midazolam (rescue medication, only if seizure >5 min — usually given as emergency, not routine OPD prescription) Valium (rectal)/Buccolam Weight-based, as per emergency protocol. For parents of children with recurrent/prolonged seizures, under specialist guidance', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Neck stiffness, bulging fontanelle, persistent altered consciousness, petechial rash → suspect meningitis/encephalitis, urgent workup (consider LP), admit
Seizure duration >15 minutes, focal features, or recurrence within 24 hours (complex febrile seizure) → needs more thorough workup, consider admission
Age <6 months or >5 years with first ''febrile seizure'' → reconsider diagnosis, more likely underlying pathology
Not returning to normal neurological baseline after seizure resolves

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE CKS and AAP guidelines: simple febrile seizures (generalised, <15 min, non-recurrent within 24h) in a neurologically normal child with an identified benign fever source do not require neuroimaging, EEG, or lumbar puncture as routine; reassurance and fever source management are key; recurrence risk is about 30%, and antiepileptic prophylaxis is NOT recommended for simple febrile seizures given the excellent prognosis and low risk of subsequent epilepsy.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Reassure the family this is common and usually outgrown by age 5-6, teach basic seizure first-aid, treat fever for comfort, seek emergency care if a seizure lasts more than 5 minutes or the child doesn''t return to normal afterward.', NULL, true, false),
('Bronchiolitis', 'Pediatrics', NULL, NULL, 'Infant, Wheeze, Viral', 'Wheezy, snuffly infants under 1 year — mostly viral and mostly needs nothing but time and support.', 'Age typically <12 months (peak 3–6 months), coryzal symptoms progressing to cough, wheeze, and increased work of breathing over 1–3 days
Difficulty feeding (a key marker of severity in infants)
Apnoeic episodes reported by parents (concerning, especially in young/premature infants)
Prematurity, congenital heart/lung disease, immunodeficiency — increase severity risk
Contact with older siblings/family with cold symptoms (RSV is highly contagious)', 'Respiratory rate, work of breathing: nasal flaring, subcostal/intercostal recession, head bobbing
Auscultation: fine crackles and/or high-pitched wheeze bilaterally
Pulse oximetry — key objective severity marker
Assess feeding adequacy and hydration status
Apnoea history is a red flag even if not observed in clinic', 'Usually a clinical diagnosis — investigations not routinely required
Pulse oximetry essential in every assessment
Nasopharyngeal swab for RSV/viral PCR only if it will change management (e.g. cohorting in hospital)
Chest X-ray NOT routinely indicated (does not change management in typical bronchiolitis)', NULL, 'Supportive care is the mainstay: this is a KEY teaching point — bronchodilators, steroids, and antibiotics are NOT recommended by NICE for typical bronchiolitis
Ensure adequate feeding — smaller, more frequent feeds if struggling; NG feeding or IV fluids if intake inadequate
Nasal suction for secretions if obstructing feeding/breathing
Oxygen therapy only if SpO2 persistently below 92%
Safety-net advice is crucial given the typically self-limiting but sometimes progressive course over days 3-5 of illness

PRESCRIPTION:
Supportive care only (no routine drug therapy) — — —. NICE explicitly recommends AGAINST salbutamol, steroids, and antibiotics for typical bronchiolitis — resist the urge to prescribe them
Paracetamol Syrup (only if fever causing distress) Calpol Syrup 10-15 mg/kg QDS PRN. For comfort only, does not alter disease course
Saline nasal drops (symptomatic relief of congestion) Nasomist/Sinomarin saline drops 1-2 drops each nostril before feeds. Helps clear secretions to aid feeding', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Apnoea (observed or reported), grunting, marked chest recession, SpO2 <92%, poor feeding (<50% normal intake), or looking exhausted/lethargic → admit
Age <3 months (higher risk of severe disease/apnoea)
Underlying comorbidity (prematurity, congenital heart disease, chronic lung disease, immunodeficiency) → lower threshold for admission

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE NG9 (Bronchiolitis in children): bronchiolitis is a clinical diagnosis; do NOT routinely use bronchodilators (salbutamol), steroids, or antibiotics — none have proven benefit in trials despite being commonly prescribed; management is supportive — oxygen if SpO2 persistently <92%, fluids/feeding support (NG tube or IV if needed), and monitoring; most infants recover fully within 2 weeks with supportive care alone.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Explain to parents this is a viral illness that runs its course over about 2 weeks, symptoms often peak around day 3-5 before improving; return immediately if the baby struggles to feed, breathes very fast/hard, or has any pauses in breathing.', NULL, true, false),
('Protein-Energy Malnutrition (SAM/MAM)', 'Pediatrics', NULL, NULL, 'Growth, Nutrition, Stunting', 'Pakistan has among the highest child stunting/wasting rates globally — every OPD visit is a chance to weigh and plot.', 'Feeding history: exclusive breastfeeding duration, complementary feeding introduction and adequacy, dietary diversity
Recurrent infections (diarrhoea, respiratory infections) — both cause and consequence of malnutrition
Birth weight, growth trend if previous records available
Household food security, maternal nutritional status, number/spacing of siblings
Appetite, recent illness, oedema (swelling of feet — sign of kwashiorkor/severe acute malnutrition)', 'Plot weight-for-age, height/length-for-age, and weight-for-height/MUAC (mid-upper arm circumference) on WHO growth charts
MUAC <11.5cm (6-59 months) = severe acute malnutrition; 11.5-12.5cm = moderate
Bilateral pitting oedema — automatically classifies as severe acute malnutrition regardless of weight
Signs of micronutrient deficiency: pallor (anaemia), angular stomatitis, hair changes, skin changes
Assess for medical complications: appetite test, signs of infection, dehydration, hypoglycaemia signs', 'Anthropometry is the core ''investigation'': weight, height/length, MUAC, oedema check, plotted on WHO charts
Haemoglobin/CBC to check for anaemia
Blood glucose if lethargic (hypoglycaemia common in SAM)
Screen for underlying causes: stool exam if chronic diarrhoea, TB screening if indicated', NULL, 'Uncomplicated SAM: outpatient RUTF (Plumpy''Nut) per weight, routine antibiotic course (even without obvious infection, per WHO protocol), vitamin A, folic acid, weekly follow-up for weight gain monitoring
Complicated SAM (with medical complication): admit for WHO 10-step inpatient management
Moderate acute malnutrition: supplementary feeding (fortified blended foods), nutrition counselling, more frequent follow-up
Deworming, immunisation catch-up, and micronutrient supplementation as part of comprehensive care
Maternal/family nutrition education — dietary diversity, continued breastfeeding, hygiene practices

PRESCRIPTION:
Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food (RUTF) Plumpy''Nut (through National Nutrition Programme/Pakistan Nutrition Support) ~200 kcal/kg/day divided through the day, per weight-based sachet count. Community-based management of uncomplicated SAM
Amoxicillin (routine antibiotic per WHO SAM protocol) Amoxil Syrup 25 mg/kg BD for 5 days. Given routinely in SAM even without overt infection signs, per WHO
Vitamin A + Folic Acid + Multivitamin/mineral mix Vitamin A capsule (EPI), Folic acid tablets, Paediatric multivitamin syrup Per WHO/national protocol by age/weight. Part of standard SAM micronutrient package
Deworming (Albendazole, if >1 year and not given recently) Zentel 400 mg single dose. Per national deworming schedule', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Severe acute malnutrition (MUAC <11.5cm or WHZ <-3 or bilateral oedema) WITH a medical complication (poor appetite, infection, lethargy, oedema, or age <6 months) → admit for inpatient stabilisation
Severe acute malnutrition without complications and passing appetite test → can be managed as outpatient with ready-to-use therapeutic food (RUTF), but needs close follow-up
Any malnourished child with diarrhoea, pneumonia, or fever needs careful management — standard doses/fluid protocols differ in SAM

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per WHO guidelines for the management of severe acute malnutrition: uncomplicated SAM (passes appetite test, no medical complications) is managed in the community with Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food (RUTF) — Pakistan''s Nutrition Programme distributes this as ''Plumpy''Nut''; complicated SAM requires inpatient stabilisation following the WHO 10-step approach (treat/prevent hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, dehydration, correct electrolyte imbalance, treat infection, correct micronutrient deficiencies, cautious feeding, catch-up growth, sensory stimulation, prepare for follow-up); moderate acute malnutrition managed with supplementary feeding programmes.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Weigh and plot growth at every visit, continue/restart breastfeeding wherever possible, weekly follow-up until adequate weight gain trend established, refer for inpatient care immediately if the child develops poor appetite, oedema, or any infection sign.', NULL, true, false),
('Acute Pharyngitis / Tonsillitis', 'Pediatrics', NULL, NULL, 'Sore throat, Fever, Strep', 'Sore throat and fever in a child — most are viral, but knowing when to treat for Strep matters for rheumatic fever prevention in Pakistan.', 'Sore throat, pain on swallowing, fever, reduced oral intake
Absence of cough/coryza (suggests bacterial/streptococcal cause more than viral)
Contact with similar illness, previous episodes of tonsillitis/quinsy
Ask about joint pains, rash — relevant given rheumatic fever risk in under-treated streptococcal pharyngitis in Pakistan
Difficulty breathing, drooling, muffled voice — concerning for peritonsillar abscess/airway compromise', 'Inspect throat: tonsillar erythema, exudate, palatal petechiae
Palpate anterior cervical lymph nodes for tender lymphadenopathy
Use Centor/McIsaac criteria (fever, tonsillar exudate, tender anterior cervical nodes, absence of cough) to estimate streptococcal likelihood
Check for trismus, uvular deviation, muffled ''hot potato'' voice (suggests peritonsillar abscess)
Assess hydration status given reduced oral intake', 'Usually a clinical diagnosis using Centor/McIsaac scoring
Throat swab/rapid strep test if available and score suggests intermediate probability
Full blood count if diagnosis unclear or systemically unwell
Monospot/EBV serology if glandular fever suspected (marked lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, prolonged course)', NULL, 'Supportive care for most cases: analgesia/antipyretics, adequate fluids, warm salt-water gargles for older children
Antibiotics if Centor/McIsaac score suggests high streptococcal likelihood, or in settings/populations with high rheumatic fever risk
Complete the full antibiotic course when prescribed — critical for rheumatic fever prevention, not just symptom relief
Refer urgently if peritonsillar abscess or airway compromise suspected
Consider ENT referral for recurrent severe tonsillitis (criteria-based) for possible tonsillectomy

PRESCRIPTION:
Penicillin V (Phenoxymethylpenicillin) — first-line for confirmed/likely Strep Pen-V/Distaquaine 250-500mg QDS (per weight in children) for 10 days. Full 10-day course essential for rheumatic fever prevention, even if symptoms resolve early
Amoxicillin (alternative, better palatability as syrup) Amoxil Syrup 25 mg/kg BD for 10 days. Avoid if glandular fever/EBV suspected (risk of rash)
Paracetamol Syrup (analgesia/antipyretic) Calpol/Panadol Syrup 10-15mg/kg QDS PRN. For pain/fever relief', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Trismus, uvular deviation, drooling, muffled voice → suspect peritonsillar (quinsy) or retropharyngeal abscess, urgent ENT referral
Stridor or significant respiratory distress → airway emergency
Signs of systemic illness (high fever, rash, joint pain) → consider scarlet fever or evolving rheumatic fever, needs prompt antibiotic treatment and follow-up
Inability to swallow fluids leading to dehydration

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE CKS and WHO/local rheumatic fever prevention guidance (particularly relevant given Pakistan''s continued burden of rheumatic heart disease): use a clinical scoring system (Centor/McIsaac) to guide antibiotic decisions — high scores (3-4) warrant antibiotics to reduce complications including rheumatic fever; penicillin/amoxicillin is first-line if bacterial cause likely; most sore throats are viral and self-limiting, but the threshold for treating suspected streptococcal pharyngitis is lower in populations with high rheumatic fever prevalence.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Complete the full 10-day antibiotic course if prescribed — stopping early is a key preventable cause of rheumatic fever in children here. Encourage fluids and soft foods, warm salt-water gargles for older children, return if breathing becomes difficult or swallowing becomes impossible.', NULL, true, false),
('Intestinal Worm Infestation', 'Pediatrics', NULL, NULL, 'Worms, Nutrition, Common', 'Perianal itching, poor appetite, and pot-belly — extremely common given sanitation patterns; part of routine deworming programmes.', 'Perianal itching (especially at night — classic for pinworm/Enterobius), disturbed sleep
Abdominal pain/distension, poor appetite, visible worms in stool (roundworm - Ascaris - may be seen)
Poor growth/weight gain, pallor (chronic hookworm causes iron-deficiency anaemia)
Sanitation/hygiene practices at home, barefoot walking (relevant for hookworm transmission), overcrowding
Previous deworming history and compliance with national deworming programme', 'Growth parameters (weight, height) — chronic infestation affects nutrition
Pallor if hookworm-related anaemia
Abdominal exam for distension, mass (heavy Ascaris load rarely causes obstruction)
Perianal inspection for excoriation from scratching (pinworm)
General nutritional assessment', 'Usually a clinical diagnosis, especially in endemic/high-prevalence settings — empirical deworming is common practice
Stool microscopy for ova/parasites if diagnosis uncertain or treatment failure
CBC if anaemia suspected (hookworm)
Perianal ''tape test'' in the morning if pinworm specifically suspected and diagnosis unclear', NULL, 'Single-dose albendazole or mebendazole for the affected child
For pinworm (Enterobius): treat the whole household simultaneously and repeat dose after 2 weeks to catch the reinfection cycle, wash bedding/clothing in hot water
Address contributing factors: footwear to prevent hookworm entry, hand hygiene, safe stool disposal, deworming as part of routine national programme participation
Treat associated iron-deficiency anaemia if present (common with hookworm)
Nutritional support/counselling if growth has been affected

PRESCRIPTION:
Albendazole Zentel/Albend 400 mg single dose (200mg if age 1-2 years), repeat after 2 weeks for pinworm. Well-tolerated, broad-spectrum against most soil-transmitted helminths
Mebendazole (alternative) Vermox 100 mg BD for 3 days, or 500mg single dose. Alternative anthelmintic, similar spectrum
Ferrous Sulfate (if anaemic from hookworm) Ferrous Sulphate Syrup/Fefol Per weight-based paediatric dosing. Continue for 3 months after Hb normalises', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Signs of intestinal obstruction (severe abdominal pain, distension, vomiting, absolute constipation) from heavy Ascaris worm load → surgical emergency, refer immediately
Severe anaemia from chronic hookworm infestation → investigate and treat anaemia alongside deworming
Failure to thrive/severe malnutrition attributable to chronic infestation → nutritional assessment and support needed alongside treatment

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per WHO guidelines on preventive chemotherapy for soil-transmitted helminths: mass deworming with albendazole or mebendazole is recommended periodically (typically twice yearly) for children in endemic areas as a public health measure, alongside health education on hygiene and sanitation; individual symptomatic children should be treated with a single-dose anthelmintic, with treatment of the whole household considered for pinworm given its person-to-person transmission via eggs.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Treat the whole household together for pinworm and repeat the dose after 2 weeks, wash hands before meals and after toilet use, wear footwear outdoors, trim nails short, wash bedding/underwear in hot water during treatment, participate in school-based deworming programmes.', NULL, true, false),
('Vitamin D Deficiency Rickets', 'Pediatrics', NULL, NULL, 'Bone health, Nutrition, Toddlers', 'Bowed legs and delayed walking in a child kept mostly indoors or covered — very common despite abundant sunshine in Pakistan.', 'Delayed walking/motor milestones, bone pain, leg deformity (bow legs/knock knees) noticed by parents
Poor growth, dental delay, frequent respiratory infections (associated with deficiency)
Limited sun exposure (cultural clothing practices, indoor lifestyle), exclusively breastfed without vitamin D supplementation, dark skin (reduces cutaneous synthesis)
Dietary history — low intake of vitamin D/calcium-rich foods
Maternal vitamin D status during pregnancy (maternal deficiency predisposes to infantile rickets)', 'Bowing of legs (genu varum) or knock knees (genu valgum) depending on age at onset
Widened wrists and ankles, rachitic rosary (beading at costochondral junctions), craniotabes (softened skull) in infants
Delayed closure of fontanelle, frontal bossing
Growth parameters — often shorter stature for age
Muscle weakness/hypotonia, waddling gait in older children', 'Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D (the key diagnostic test — low level confirms deficiency)
Serum calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase (typically raised in active rickets), and parathyroid hormone
X-ray of wrist/knee — shows characteristic cupping, fraying, and widening of the metaphyses
Renal function tests if a renal cause is suspected (non-response to standard treatment)', NULL, 'High-dose vitamin D replacement therapy for a defined treatment course, followed by a lower maintenance/prophylactic dose
Ensure adequate dietary calcium intake alongside vitamin D (dairy products, or calcium supplementation if intake poor)
Encourage safe, regular sun exposure (arms/legs, short daily periods) as a sustainable long-term source
Monitor for healing on repeat X-ray and normalisation of biochemistry (calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase) after treatment course
Screen and treat maternal vitamin D deficiency in future pregnancies/lactation to prevent recurrence in siblings

PRESCRIPTION:
Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol) — high-dose treatment course Vidrop/D-Rise/Calcirol 2000-4000 IU/day for 3 months, OR a single high-dose ''stoss'' therapy (e.g. 100,000-300,000 IU) under medical supervision. Treatment dose is higher than routine prophylactic supplementation — follow weight/age-based national protocol
Calcium Carbonate/Calcium supplementation Calcimax Syrup/Ostocalcium Syrup Per weight, divided doses. Given alongside vitamin D, especially if dietary calcium intake is poor
Vitamin D3 (maintenance/prophylactic dose after treatment course) Vidrop drops 400 IU/day ongoing. For prevention of recurrence, especially in exclusively breastfed infants', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Hypocalcaemic seizures or tetany (can occur, especially in infants with severe deficiency) → medical emergency, urgent calcium correction
Severe skeletal deformity affecting mobility → orthopaedic referral may be needed alongside medical treatment
Failure to improve despite adequate treatment → consider rarer causes (renal tubular disorders, vitamin D-resistant rickets), refer to paediatric endocrinology
Signs of associated severe malnutrition

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per WHO/UNICEF guidance and national programmes on vitamin D deficiency rickets: treatment involves high-dose vitamin D replacement (either daily or as an intermittent ''stoss'' high-dose regimen) plus adequate calcium intake; prevention relies on vitamin D supplementation for exclusively breastfed infants, encouraging safe sun exposure, and maternal vitamin D optimisation during pregnancy/lactation, given how common deficiency remains despite Pakistan''s high sunshine hours (largely due to cultural covering practices and indoor lifestyles).

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Complete the full high-dose treatment course as prescribed, encourage short daily periods of safe sun exposure (arms and legs), include dairy/calcium-rich foods in the diet, and ensure any future siblings receive routine vitamin D supplementation from early infancy.', NULL, true, false);
